YAML is a simple text file used to tell tools and platforms what steps and configuration they should follow.

======

YAML — What is it?

YAML is a human-readable configuration file format.

YAML stands for:

YAML Ain't Markup Language

It is commonly used to define configuration, automation, CI/CD pipelines, Docker-related setups, and cloud deployments.

Simple YAML example
name: Sugumar


skills:
  - Python
  - FastAPI
  - Google Cloud

This means:

Name: Sugumar


Skills:
• Python
• FastAPI
• Google Cloud

=====

Why YAML is important in CI/CD?

For example, a GitHub Actions workflow might use:

name: Deploy Application


on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main


jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest


    steps:
      - name: Get Code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4


      - name: Deploy
        run: echo "Deploying application..."

======

What happens here?
Push code to GitHub
        ↓
GitHub reads YAML file
        ↓
Starts CI/CD workflow
        ↓
Runs the defined steps
        ↓
Build / Test / Deploy 🚀

======

YAML vs JSON

| Feature            | YAML                   | JSON                 |
| ------------------ | ---------------------- | -------------------- |
| Easy for humans    | ✅ Very easy            | Medium               |
| Uses `{ }`         | ❌ Usually no           | ✅ Yes                |
| Uses indentation   | ✅ Yes                  | ❌ No                 |
| Comments supported | ✅ Yes                  | ❌ No                 |
| Common use         | Configurations & CI/CD | APIs & data exchange |


=========

Important YAML rule

Indentation is very important.

Correct:

app:
  name: MyApp
  port: 8080

Incorrect indentation can cause errors:

app:
name: MyApp
  port: 8080

